## Method -- why no mirror status output is used as proof
Group 1 established three defects in this platform's mirroring, all measured there:
  M1  a mirror RE-APPLIED to a port that has previously held one copies NOTHING until that
      destination port is bounced (shutdown / no shutdown), while `show mirror` reports it
      Enabled and correct throughout;
  M2  an in-place `direction` change is SILENTLY IGNORED and `show mirror` keeps reporting the
      OLD direction, which the dataplane also keeps using;
  M3  `direction both` copies no frames at all while every status command reports it correctly.
So on this platform `show mirror` cannot be read as a statement about the dataplane. Every
claim below rests on frames captured at tb277 eth3, and the M1 bounce is applied before any
measurement. `show mirror` is quoted only to record what the switch reported.

## Tools
cfg.py, ckcon.py from tools/; `cap.sh` (carried from group 1) runs tcpdump on eth3 across a
6-ping flow and counts echo REQUESTS (ingress on port1.0.48) against echo REPLIES (egress).
Raw transcripts in ../work-box/ (commit dec3d5c).

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 1 -- make port1.0.10 an ACL-driven mirror destination
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface none
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL)       <-- the ACL-driven form; this is the mechanism
                                                copy-to-mirror targets, not an "off" switch
Then, per defect M1, the destination port was bounced:
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10 ; shutdown ; no shutdown
  => STEP 1 PASS: the destination is configured as ACL-driven and has been bounced, so a later
     absence of frames cannot be attributed to M1.

### STEP 2 -- D0 baseline: no copy-to-mirror rule yet
host# ./cap.sh t44990-d0
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0 ; ping 6/6, 0% packet loss
  => STEP 2 PASS: traffic flows and the destination captures nothing. Anything captured later
     is attributable to the copy-to-mirror rule.

### STEP 3 -- copy-to-mirror: BOTH halves of the guide's definition
The ACL guide defines copy-to-mirror as "copy to the mirror port AND forward". Proving only
the mirror half would not distinguish it from send-to-mirror, which does not forward.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44990
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group t44990
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
  Hardware IP access list t44990
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
   access-group t44990

host# ./cap.sh t44990-a
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 6 ; echo REPLIES = 0      <-- HALF ONE: the matching frames arrived
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss   <-- HALF TWO: still forwarded
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44990
      Packet Hits: 6                        <-- exactly the 6 frames captured
  => STEP 3 PASS: matching frames were copied to the mirror destination AND were still
     forwarded to the far side. The agreement between the ACL hit count and the captured
     frame count is what makes the mirror half positive proof. Only echo REQUESTS appear,
     never replies, which is correct: the rule matches src .65 -> dst .70, which is the
     request direction only -- so the capture also demonstrates that the rule selects.

### STEP 4 -- copy-to-mirror TERMINATES ACL processing at the first match
This is the documented behaviour and the reason `mirror interface <src> vlan <vid>` was later
added to the product. Tested with one pair of rules, changing only their ORDER.

  A) deny placed AFTER the copy-to-mirror:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#8 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      8 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- the deny was NEVER EVALUATED

  B) CONTROL -- the same deny placed BEFORE the copy-to-mirror:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 8
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
      1 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  100% packet loss                          <-- now the deny bites
host# ./cap.sh t44990-denyfirst
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0  <-- and NOTHING is mirrored either

  => STEP 4 PASS: the identical two rules, with only their order changed, give opposite
     outcomes on BOTH the forwarding and the mirroring. With copy-to-mirror first, the later
     deny is never reached and traffic flows. With the deny first, the copy-to-mirror is never
     reached and nothing is mirrored. Processing stops at the first match -- exactly as
     documented -- and the control rules out "the deny simply does not work".

### STEP 5 -- the named list was edited WHILE BOUND, three times
Every edit in STEP 4 was made with `access-group t44990` still on port1.0.48. Each produced
  % This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied
    to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode ...
and each edit DID take effect, as the opposite ping results show. This confirms T44979's
finding F1 from a second, independent direction: for a NAMED list this `%` line is an
ADVISORY and the edit applies, whereas a NUMBERED list refuses the same edit outright
(T44987 F1). Note that tools/cfg.py treats the advisory as non-fatal and continues; before
that fix it would have aborted the batch and silently skipped the remaining commands.

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the copy-to-mirror action, bound to a port, copies
matching frames to the ACL-driven mirror destination (6 captured at the destination, matching
its 6 ACL hits exactly) AND still forwards them to the far side (0% packet loss), which is the
guide's definition of the action. Only the direction the rule names is copied. ACL processing
terminates at the copy-to-mirror match: a deny placed after it is never evaluated and traffic
flows, while the same deny placed before it blocks the traffic and suppresses the mirroring --
the documented first-match behaviour, proven by an order-only pair. All mirror evidence is
frame-level, with the destination port bounced beforehand, because group 1 established that
this platform's mirror status output does not reliably describe the dataplane.
